# The plugin's own cron.d file, not core's Cron: the health mirror ticks every
# 15 s, and core's Cron schedules by the minute at best.

# WireGuard tunnel health mirror (wgct.sh tick): holds each tunnel down while
# its bound WAN is down, and each IPv6 tunnel gateway down while its own IPv4
# tunnel is unhealthy. After each tick, prime.php sends one TCP connect from a
# tunnel's IPv6 address when its IPv6 gateway is down while its WireGuard
# session is up (a handshake within 180 s): the provider learns the address to
# answer from such traffic, and dpinger's pings never teach it.
* * * * *	root	/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/OPNsense/WGClientTunnels/wgct.sh tick
* * * * *	root	sleep 15 && /usr/local/opnsense/scripts/OPNsense/WGClientTunnels/wgct.sh tick
* * * * *	root	sleep 30 && /usr/local/opnsense/scripts/OPNsense/WGClientTunnels/wgct.sh tick
* * * * *	root	sleep 45 && /usr/local/opnsense/scripts/OPNsense/WGClientTunnels/wgct.sh tick

# Route reconciliation safety net. The event hooks in plugins.inc.d cover the
# known ways the gateway host routes get dropped; this catches anything that
# removes them without firing a hook, so a lost route self-heals within a minute
# instead of sitting broken until someone notices the gateway is down. It also
# runs default_guard.php, which removes any default route through a tunnel,
# freshness.php, which keeps the rendered firewall rules current,
# and core_contract.php, which reports a core update that changed what the
# plugin copies. The route set's IPv6 far-gateway host routes work around
# opnsense/core#10578.
* * * * *	root	/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/OPNsense/WGClientTunnels/wgct.sh reconcile --cron
